  prose(<<~'CANONICAL_PROSE', origin: :published)
    ## Definition and Nature
    
    Threk Boneday is an orca war-ritual classified by penguin field scholars as one of the orca "awakenings" — large-scale pressure-depth workings conducted in the deep waters above the Berg's oldest ice strata. The term *celebration* appears in some penguin catalogues and is misleading. The ritual is a synchronized breaking: a coordinated channeling of blood-magic through bone arranged in spiral patterns that mirror the Berg's own pressure-rings.
    
    The ritual requires bone — whale, seal, and penguin, the latter harvested across multiple shore-raids according to the current active description, though an earlier account notes penguin bone was used only "when the hunts go well." Each bone serves as a conduit for pressure-depth communion. Shamans chant at frequencies reported to produce visible disturbance in surrounding ice.
    
    ## The First Observance
    
    The first great Threk Boneday was conducted by Thung Thrakrek, the orca war-shaman, during the Orca Incursion's darkest phase. The ritual produced **Threk's Opening** — not a discrete technique but a binding that synchronized will across scattered pods that would otherwise have fought among themselves. The canonical account holds that three shaman-pods lost their minds in the working. The shamans who conducted it never surfaced again.
    
    Orcas claim Thung Thrakrek's voice alone cracked ice that had not broken since the Great Thaw. This is, of course, an orca claim.
    
    ## Observable Effects and Persistence
    
    Penguin observers on the surface reported aurora-lights flickering during the ritual. Accounts diverge on severity: earlier sources say the lights "dimmed"; later versions assert the sky went entirely dark for a count of sixty. The interrogation of blood-speaker initiate Kzund-threk-zaith — conducted by Interrogator Gork Slaughterend — confirms the binding was designed for a single synchronization, meant to expire when the shamans' minds broke. It did not. Bone fragments recovered from subsequent engagements carry residual frequency, and ice at contested sites retains impressions the ritual's intended duration cannot account for.
    
    Surviving practitioners bear a ritual split in the dorsal fin, each scar mapping the number of breakings endured.
    
    ## Contested Legacy
    
    Some Aurora Stack historians claim **the Shell Hatch**, an aurora-channeled ice-memory reading technique, was reverse-engineered from the ritual's echo-patterns. The implication — that orca pressure-magic taught penguins to weaponize the Berg's memory — remains unverified and, in certain circles, unpopular.
    
    The orcas speak of a phase or consequence that follows Threk Boneday, for which they have a word and penguins do not. The ice at the relevant depths offers no clarification. It seldom does when you need it to.
  CANONICAL_PROSE
  annotation "note_1771586599950_0", anchor: "the sky went entirely dark for a count of sixty", text: "Sixty. Counted by whom — a penguin surface observer timing an atmospheric event during a deep-sea orca ritual? And sixty, not fifty-three, not sixty-one. The earlier 'dimmed' has the shape of something someone actually saw. Sixty has the shape of something someone wanted to say at a feast.", type: :skepticism, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771586599950_1", anchor: "orca pressure-magic taught penguins to weaponize the Berg's memory", text: "The comedy here is architectural. Penguin communion techniques were folded into orca killing doctrine through the Grend Blood-Arts. Threk's Opening consumed the Blood-Arts and their penguin inheritance. Now Aurora scholars propose the Shell Hatch was reverse-engineered from the Opening's echo-patterns — which means penguins may have recovered their own pressure-magic, laundered through two generations of orca ritual, and called it a breakthrough. The magic went out through blood and came back through ice and nobody on either side wants to trace the full circuit.", type: :commentary, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771586599950_2", anchor: "when the hunts go well", text: "The earlier account is from a pod-song fragment recovered at depth. 'When the hunts go well' is a conditional — the ritual was designed to function without penguin bone. That it now requires it tells you something about what the ritual became after Thung Thrakrek, not what it was before.", type: :correction, display: :popout
